// window/x11/bind/xkblib.cst — XKBlib.h: the keyboard, properly.
//
// The core protocol's model of a keyboard is a table from keycode to a short
// list of keysyms, and it cannot express most of what a modern keyboard does:
// which physical key a keycode belongs to, what a layout group is, how a
// modifier is derived, what happens on the third level, or how to detect a
// key repeat as distinct from a fast press-release-press.
//
// XKB replaces all of it, and it is the largest single subsystem in libX11 —
// 120 of the 774 functions, more than the entire drawing API.
//
// What ../keys.cst will want from here, and does not use yet:
//
//   XkbKeycodeToKeysym       the non-deprecated form of the lookup keys.cst
//                            currently does with XKeycodeToKeysym; the two
//                            agree for the unshifted level, which is all it
//                            reads today
//   XkbSetDetectableAutoRepeat  without it, a held key produces an endless
//                            KeyRelease/KeyPress pair per repeat and a program
//                            cannot tell a repeat from a real release
//   XkbGetState              the layout group, for a program that shows it
//   XkbKeycodeToPhysical     via the geometry in xkbgeom.cst, the answer to
//                            "which key is where", which is what a game means
//                            by WASD
//
// Bound in full now so that reaching for any of them later is a call rather
// than a change to the binding.

